    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The first stone church in the islands, Wainee Church, was built in the late 1820s and later renamed Waiola Church in 1953.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/history-lahaina-hawaii"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Lahaina’s harbor hosted the whaling industry from 1819 to the 1860s, turning the town into a bustling whaling hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/history-lahaina-hawaii"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1831 missionaries founded Lahainaluna Seminary, now Lahainaluna High School, where Hawaiian boys learned crafts like printing and carpentry.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lahaina%2C_Hawaii"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The historic banyan tree in Banyan Tree Park, planted in the 19th century, now spans 1,000 square feet and is a landmark of Lahaina.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
